-- title:
--   Prop. 3, p. 12 — $Z^\tau_i$ is a uniformly integrable martingale and $\mathbb E[Z^{T_i}_i]=0$
-- statement:
--   Let $(\mu,\Sigma)$ be a belief and $i$ an arm with $\lambda_i>0$ and $\Sigma_{i,i}>0$. On the auxiliary space of repeated sampling of arm $i$, let $Z^\tau_i$ be the change in arm $i$'s posterior mean after $\tau$ samples and $(\mathcal F_\tau)$ the filtration generated by the samples. Then:
--   1. $(Z^\tau_i)_{\tau\ge0}$ is a martingale with respect to $(\mathcal F_\tau)$;
--   2. the family $\{Z^\tau_i:\tau\ge0\}$ is uniformly integrable;
--   3. for every stopping time $T$ of $(\mathcal F_\tau)$ that is finite almost surely,
--
--   $$
--   \mathbb E\big[Z^{T}_i\big]=0 .
--   $$
--
--   This is what removes the linear term $b_{g(0)}Z^{T_i}_i$ from the stopping problem in (18), giving (19)–(20).
--
--   **Formalization Note** The paper's "any stopping time" is read as any almost surely finite stopping time, since $Z^{T}_i$ is only defined where $T<\infty$. Uniform integrability is Mathlib's notion, which includes measurability and boundedness in $L^1$.
-- source:
--   Chick, Gans, Yapar, Bayesian Sequential Learning for Clinical Trials of Multiple Correlated Medical Interventions, SSRN 3184758 (version of August 31, 2020), p. 12, Prop. 3 (proof p. ec3, B.3)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_CorrelatedTrials_Consistency_AllocationIndex

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

namespace CorrelatedTrials.Consistency

/-- Prop. 3 (Chick, Gans, Yapar, Bayesian Sequential Learning for Clinical Trials of Multiple Correlated Medical Interventions, SSRN 3184758 (version of August 31, 2020), p. 12, Prop. 3; proof p. ec3):
"Z^τ_i is a uniformly integrable martingale. E_{T_i}[Z^{T_i}_i | µ^t, Σ^t] = 0 for any stopping
time T_i."

From a belief with `Σ_{i,i} > 0`, the predictive change `Z^τ_i` of arm `i`'s mean under repeated
sampling of arm `i` is a martingale for the sampling filtration, the family `(Z^τ_i)_τ` is
uniformly integrable, and `E[Z^{T}_i] = 0` for every stopping time `T`.

Formalization Note: "any stopping time" is read as every almost surely finite stopping time
(`Z^T` needs `T < ∞`; Lean's `stoppedValue` would read `Z^0` on `{T = ∞}`). Mathlib's
`UniformIntegrable` includes strong measurability and boundedness in `L¹`. -/
theorem proposition_3 {M : ℕ} (lam : Fin M → ℝ) (Sig : Matrix (Fin M) (Fin M) ℝ) (i : Fin M)
    (hlam : 0 < lam i) (hSig : 0 < Sig i i) :
    Martingale (Zproc (lam i) (Sig i i)) (auxFiltration (lam i)) (auxMeasure (Sig i i)) ∧
      UniformIntegrable (Zproc (lam i) (Sig i i)) 1 (auxMeasure (Sig i i)) ∧
      ∀ T : AuxSpace → WithTop ℕ, IsStoppingTime (auxFiltration (lam i)) T →
        (∀ᵐ ω ∂(auxMeasure (Sig i i)), T ω ≠ ⊤) →
        ∫ ω, stoppedValue (Zproc (lam i) (Sig i i)) T ω ∂(auxMeasure (Sig i i)) = 0 := by sorry

end CorrelatedTrials.Consistency
